WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
When I say "we", you say "work".

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:03.760
We!

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:04.480
Work!

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:04.980
We!

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:05.500
Work!

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:07.880
That’s the charismatic CEO of WeWork,

00:00:07.880 --> 00:00:09.060
Adam Neumann

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.820
If you’re doing something that has intention

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:12.980
or meaning in it,

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:14.260
you cannot fail.

00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:17.280
The feeling you have right now of being together,

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:20.340
the feeling of being happy - that’s what WeWork is!

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:23.642
It is an idea that's shared by a lot of startups,

00:00:23.642 --> 00:00:26.160
that your work should be more than just something

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:28.720
that you do 9 to 5, Monday through Friday

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.520
but really a calling or a mission,

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.360
or something that you feel truly compelled to do.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:40.300
WeWork believes that they’re not just
selling a workplace,

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.680
but a community.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:44.740
They really think of this idea of the community

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.400
as something not measurable but very effective

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:51.440
in giving the workplace a sort of spark of energy.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.660
And Neumann has a term for this intangible energy.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.780
I'm pretty sure he would call it "the power of 'We'".

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:03.140
This is the story of how WeWork turned work into 
a lifestyle

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.100
and wants to change the world next.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.900
The co-working giant WeWork is amongst

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.520
the most valuable startups in the world

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:16.140
but 2019 hasn’t started as the company may
have been hoping.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:19.040
Now WeWork is not working for SoftBank.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.540
The Japanese conglomerate announced Monday it’s investing an additional $2 billion

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.260
in the co-working startup.

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.600
It's a step back from the $16 billion investment that would have given

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.000
SoftBank a controlling stake in WeWork.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:38.060
The significantly smaller investment from
Japan’s SoftBank leaves WeWork more vulnerable

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.200
to a potential economic downturn

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.500
or a slump in lease prices.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.280
Still, WeWork is plowing ahead

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.300
and announced in January it’s rebranding itself as

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.760
the We Company with the ambitious mission “to
elevate the world’s consciousness”.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:02:00.280
That kind of sounds like a self-help cult now
so what does the We Company do?

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.940
The We Company will consist of three main verticals,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.100
WeWork, WeLive, which for now is just two buildings

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:08.960
for communal living,

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:15.420
and WeGrow - which is a $42,000 a year elementary school in New York.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.280
When Miguel and I started this company, the intention
was - can we change the world?

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.560
The idea for WeWork emerged from the upbringings
of Neumann and his co-founder Miguel McKelvey.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.820
They both spent part of their childhoods in 
communal living.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.480
It was really community and bringing people
together that we were both attracted to.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.100
The doors to the first WeWork location opened in 2010.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:44.780
They started by renting out desks and famously
small "fish-tank" offices to freelancers

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.400
and entrepreneurs on a monthly basis.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.000
But the WeWork model has always been about
lively communal spaces.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.000
Most WeWorks will have staff called titles
like, Community Manager

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:03.260
and they have a pretty big say in the culture of a place,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.580
they maybe organize happy hours or speaking
events or lunch time meetings that people

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.320
can come to and sort of network with each other.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.700
WeWork wants people to interact and to achieve
this they monitor how people use their spaces.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.231
We keep tremendous amount of data.

00:03:20.231 --> 00:03:22.040
Things people usually don’t talk about.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.340
Like when they get up and how often do they go to the
coffee or actually literally

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.800
connect with other people.

00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.600
WeWork has an in-house social media app used
to book rooms and rate working spaces,

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:36.540
like you would an Uber driver.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:42.020
This app, paired with data-harvesting sensors, which are spread around selected offices,

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.420
allows WeWork to observe behavior.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:50.040
Based on that, they can make design tweaks
and roll them out in their next building.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:55.480
And they have a steady stream of buildings
in development to test those ideas on.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.720
They've put a lot of energy into growing super,
super fast.

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.040
In 2014 WeWork opened its first international
location in London.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:11.620
Fast-forward five years and they have 400 locations
in 26 countries.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:18.480
They’re now the largest private tenant in
Central London, Manhattan and Washington D.C.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.120
And while it continues to expand at breakneck speed,

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.260
WeWork has also shifted its focus

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.500
to much larger enterprise customers.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:32.610
The largest companies in the world have been
approaching us, and saying hey, can you bring

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:34.600
this community into our company.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:41.120
More than 30% of WeWork’s members now work
at companies with 1000 or more employees

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:46.380
And I think this is gonna be a tricky thing
for WeWork, trying to balance the entrepreneurial

00:04:46.389 --> 00:04:50.650
energy that's a big part of their brand, with
the stability and revenue that comes from

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:53.540
these Fortune 500 and large companies.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.120
But despite all the changes, the company still
markets itself as

00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:00.200
“a community of creators”.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.720
You’re a creator and you’re a creator
and we’re all creators.

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.540
WeWork couldn’t be happier than to give
you the $50,000.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:08.320
It’s yours!

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:11.240
That’s Neumann at one of WeWork’s Creator
Awards events.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:15.260
The company launched the elevator pitch competition
in 2017,

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.740
and opened it up to people outside of
WeWork’s network.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.900
They distribute $20 million annually
in prize money

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:25.879
to “anyone with an idea and a plan”.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.680
And it’s just another way that WeWork is
trying to build its brand.

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:33.600
Like they really see this, I’m sure, as a
marketing expense.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:36.940
And that brand recognition is important

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:41.340
as the newly renamed We Company branches 
outside of office spaces.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.880
That's success...

00:05:42.920 --> 00:05:46.900
I'm not convinced yet that they're going to
have that sort of effect on the way we live.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:52.020
But yeah in terms of the way that they affect
our expectations about our workplace,

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:57.680
I do think that they've already probably made
some big changes in what companies expect

00:05:57.680 --> 00:05:59.100
and what employees expect.

00:06:00.220 --> 00:06:05.960
The core of We Company’s business is still
about adding value to the real estate they’re leasing.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:10.640
If that’s enough to weather an economic
slowdown remains to be seen.

